 ## Overview
-This family adapts 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) for X-ray imaging and radiative physics by replacing view-dependent color with view-independent scalar attributes (density or intensity) [Radiative Gaussian Splatting](3040-radiative-gaussian-splatting.md). It solves the problem of reconstructing volumetric density fields or synthesizing X-ray projections from 2D attenuation measurements, where physical attenuation depends on integrated density along the ray path rather than surface appearance [Radiative Gaussian Splatting](3040-radiative-gaussian-splatting.md). Unlike standard 3DGS, which uses spherical harmonics for view-dependent appearance, these methods remove angular dependence to model isotropic radiative transfer [Radiative Gaussian Splatting](3040-radiative-gaussian-splatting.md).
+This family adapts 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) for X-ray imaging and radiative physics by replacing view-dependent color with view-independent scalar attributes (density or intensity) . It solves the problem of reconstructing volumetric density fields or synthesizing X-ray projections from 2D attenuation measurements, where physical attenuation depends on integrated density along the ray path rather than surface appearance . Unlike standard 3DGS, which uses spherical harmonics for view-dependent appearance, these methods remove angular dependence to model isotropic radiative transfer [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All extensions in this family retain the core 3DGS primitive representation: a set of $M$ anisotropic 3D Gaussian kernels $\mathcal{G} = \{G_i\}_{i=1}^M$ [Radiative Gaussian Splatting](3040-radiative-gaussian-splatting.md). Each kernel is defined by:
-*   $\mathbf{p}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$: Position (mean) [Radiative Gaussian Splatting](3040-radiative-gaussian-splatting.md).
-*   $\mathbf{R}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$: Rotation matrix [Radiative Gaussian Splatting](3040-radiative-gaussian-splatting.md).
-*   $\mathbf{S}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$: Scale matrix [Radiative Gaussian Splatting](3040-radiative-gaussian-splatting.md).
-*   The covariance matrix is $\mathbf{\Sigma}_i = \mathbf{R}_i \mathbf{S}_i \mathbf{S}_i^\top \mathbf{R}_i^\top$ [Radiative Gaussian Splatting](3040-radiative-gaussian-splatting.md).
+All extensions in this family retain the core 3DGS primitive representation: a set of $M$ anisotropic 3D Gaussian kernels $\mathcal{G} = \{G_i\}_{i=1}^M$ . Each kernel is defined by:
+*   $\mathbf{p}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$: Position (mean) .
+*   $\mathbf{R}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$: Rotation matrix .
+*   $\mathbf{S}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$: Scale matrix .
+*   The covariance matrix is $\mathbf{\Sigma}_i = \mathbf{R}_i \mathbf{S}_i \mathbf{S}_i^\top \mathbf{R}_i^\top$ .
 
 The rendering pipeline's geometric projection remains consistent for ray-based methods: 3D Gaussians are transformed into ray space, and the projected 2D covariance $\mathbf{\hat{\Sigma}}_i$ is computed using the Jacobian $J$ of the affine approximation of the projection and the rotation part $W$ of the extrinsic matrix:
 $$ \mathbf{\hat{\Sigma}}_i = J W \mathbf{\Sigma}_i W^T J^T $$
